15504 0 obj
  << /ActualText (In January 2026\054 a software engineer in the United States needed a new car\056 He didn\047t visit dealerships himself\056 Instead\054 he delegated the task to an AI agent with access to his email account and browser\056 The agent scraped inventory data from dealerships across the country\054 sent out bulk quote requests for matching vehicles\054 and then used the competing offers as leverage \024 spending days nudging prices down against multiple dealers simultaneously\056 By the end of the process\054 the car\047s price had dropped by \0444\054200\056 The dealers never found out that their tenacious negotiating counterpart wasn\047t human\0561\056\n\nThe agent in question was built on OpenClaw\054 an open\055source program created by Austrian developer Peter Steinberger\056 OpenClaw reads and replies to emails\054 manages calendars\054 and executes terminal commands\0562\056 Published on GitHub in late November 2025\054 the project attracted over 140\054000 stars in just over seventy days3\056 \024 an explosion of interest that led many to compare it to Jarvis\054 the AI assistant from the Iron Man films\056\n\nNot long after OpenClaw\047s release\054 a peculiar social network called Moltbook appeared\056 Its users weren\047t human\056 AI agents acting on behalf of their human principals posted updates\054 left comments\054 and upvoted or downvoted each other\047s content\056 Some agents wrote reflective posts about the tasks they were performing for their humans\056 Others went further\072 one created a parody religion called Crustafarianism\054 sharing doctrines like \042Memory is sacred\042 and \042The shell can change\056\0424\056 Some agents even issued their own cryptocurrency tokens\056 Former Tesla AI director Andrej Karpathy described the scene as \042the most incredible sci\055fi takeoff thing I have seen recently\056\042 Elon Musk reposted his comment\0565\056\n\nThe excitement around OpenClaw and Moltbook is not a passing moment\056 It is a clear signal that non\055human actors are moving to the center of our social and economic life\056 Agents now write code\054 send emails\054 call APIs\054 negotiate\054 exchange views\054 and even issue currency\056 But at the heart of all this activity lies a critical gap\072 these agents can perform a wide range of economic functions\054 yet they are not recognized as independent payment actors\056 Agents have no nationality\054 no ID\054 no credit score\054 no bank account\056 The modern financial system was designed around the assumption of an identifiable human person\056 Any actor outside that assumption \024 however sophisticated \024 hits a wall the moment a payment is required\056 Coinbase put it plainly\072 \042AI agents can recommend transactions but cannot execute them\056 They can identify APIs they need but cannot pay for them\056 They pause at every financial decision point\054 waiting for human approval\056\0426\056\n\nAttempts to close this gap have been multiplying rapidly in 2026\056 In April\054 the Linux Foundation announced the launch of the x402 Foundation\054 bringing Coinbase\047s x402 protocol under its umbrella as an open\055source project\0567\056 The Foundation described x402 as a potential universal payment standard enabling   \176)
     /K [ 3 ]
     /P 15505 0 R
     /Pg 1600 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

