15528 0 obj
  << /ActualText (The web was never a finished structure\056 The early HTTP specifications that Tim Berners\055Lee and his colleagues put together in the 1990s show how the basic rules of the web we use every day came to be\056 RFC 1945 \050Request for Comments 1945\051\054 published in 1996\054 documented the then\055prevailing practices of HTTP\0571\0560\05411\056 and the following year RFC 2068 standardized HTTP\0571\0561\054 expanding the status code system1\051 that gives us familiar responses like \042404 Not Found\056\04212\056\n\nBut one code in that system went without a real use for a long time\056 That code is 402\056 Its name is \042Payment Required\042 \024 a clear signal that it was designed for situations involving payment\056 The architects standardizing HTTP\0571\0561 in the mid\0551990s anticipated that content and services requiring payment would eventually emerge\054 and so they reserved a slot in the status code system for just that purpose\056 Yet the official description attached to the code was a single line\072 \042This code is reserved for future use\056\042 The name existed\054 but how to actually implement it was left undefined\056 The web\047s blueprint had a box drawn in it for payment\054 but for a long time there was no standardized currency\054 no wallet\054 and no payment rail to fill that box\056 Code 402 sat in the middle of the status code list for nearly thirty years\054 unrealized \024 a potential waiting to be claimed\056 That description has remained essentially unchanged through RFC 9110\054 the current standard as of 2026\05613\056 This was the payment gap built into the web\047s original design\056)
     /K [ 5 ]
     /P 15529 0 R
     /Pg 2100 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

