15602 0 obj
  << /ActualText (To understand the gap x402 is trying to fill\054 we first need to ask why that gap stayed empty for so long\056 Over the thirty years since the web opened up\054 the idea of micropayments had no shortage of advocates\056 Some stayed at the level of concept\073 others pushed through to actual implementation\056 But micropayments never found a footing in the market\056 The reason wasn\047t technical failure at the project level\056 It was something far more structural\072 the pricing architecture of card networks\054 which have dominated modern payments for over seventy years\056\n\nThe modern card payment system took shape in the United States in the years after World War II\056 Diners Club introduced the first general\055purpose charge card in 1950\054 and card payments gradually became a fixture of daily life across restaurants\054 travel\054 and retail\056 From 1958 onward\054 banks began offering credit cards directly\054 and as the Visa and Mastercard networks grew\054 card payments settled into what is known as the four\055party model \024 cardholders\054 merchants\054 acquirers\054 and issuers\056 In online payments\054 payment gateways and processors were layered on top of this structure\054 handling authorization\054 security\054 and settlement between merchants and card networks\056\n\nIn this system\054 transaction fees are borne by the merchant\056 Among major processors in the US online market\054 the standard structure combines a percentage fee with a flat per\055transaction charge\056 Stripe\054 for example\054 charges 2\0569\045 plus \0440\05630 per transaction for domestic online card payments\05617\056 Sell a \04410 item and the merchant pays roughly 59 cents \024 under 6\045\054 uncomfortable but manageable\056 But run the same math on a \0440\05601 transaction and the problem becomes immediately clear\072 the merchant pays \0440\05630 in fixed fees alone to collect one cent\056 The more transactions occur\054 the deeper the loss\056 Micropayments are economically impossible on these terms\056\n\nThis isn\047t an accidental edge case \024 it\047s an inevitable consequence of how card payment pricing is structured\056 The flat per\055transaction fee acts as a floor\054 passing through transactions above a certain size while structurally excluding anything smaller\056 That floor exists because card transactions carry multiple layers of cost\072 interchange fees paid by acquirers to issuers\054 network fees\054 and acquirer margins\056 In online payments\054 gateway and processor costs and margins are added on top\056 The result\054 for merchants\054 is a fee structure that combines a percentage rate with a fixed per\055transaction charge \024 and as long as that structure holds\054 micropayments cannot work\056 The smaller the transaction\054 the more the fee eats into the value of the transaction itself\054 until at some point every additional payment makes the merchant worse off\056 For over seventy years\054 the card system has run efficiently on the assumption that transactions have a minimum meaningful size\056 Small payments fell into a             \176)
     /K [ 5 ]
     /P 15603 0 R
     /Pg 3510 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

