15615 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Attempts to engineer around this limitation kept appearing\056 Flattr\054 launched in Sweden in 2010\054 let users set a monthly support budget that would be distributed in small amounts across the websites and creators they chose to engage with\05618\056 Brave\047s Basic Attention Token \050BAT\051\054 introduced in 2017\054 proposed linking user attention\054 advertising\054 and creator rewards through a token system\05619\056 The W3C developed the Payment Request API to make existing payment methods smoother to invoke from within the browser\05620\056 None of these achieved a decisive breakthrough\056 User adoption was slow\054 and the networks of participating creators and merchants never reached critical mass\056 But the more fundamental problem lay elsewhere\056 Even when the economic barrier of card fees was lowered or bypassed\054 payment still couldn\047t escape a different kind of threshold \024 one requiring the user\047s own attention\054 approval\054 and judgment\056\n\nThe person who saw this most clearly was Nick Szabo\054 cryptographer and digital contract theorist\0566\051 In a 1999 essay titled \042Micropayments and Mental Transaction Costs\054\042 Szabo located the real barrier to micropayments not in transaction fees but somewhere else entirely\072 what he called mental transaction costs\05621\056 By this he meant the cognitive burden placed on users who must evaluate\054 compare\054 and approve or reject payments broken into extremely small units\056 The moment a user faces the question \042should I authorize this one\055cent request\054\042 a kind of mental toll is levied \024 independent of the actual payment amount\056 Processing costs can fall toward zero \024 but human attention cannot\056 People cannot endlessly repeat the cycle of pricing\054 comparing\054 and deciding\056\n\nSzabo\047s insight cuts through every subsequent micropayment experiment\056 Flattr reduced the burden of repeated payment decisions by pre\055allocating a monthly budget \024 but users still had to choose which content to support\056 BAT linked attention and rewards through tokens \024 but users still had to install the browser\054 opt into the reward system\054 and decide how to direct their attention\056 The Payment Request API simplified checkout within the browser \024 but it couldn\047t make the payment screen and the act of approval disappear\056 All of these attempts lowered the economic barrier of card fees\054 but ran into the second barrier \024 mental transaction costs\056 The granularity of the payments was simply incompatible with the speed of the web\056)
     /K [ 4 ]
     /P 15616 0 R
     /Pg 3948 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

