15628 0 obj
  << /ActualText (payment decisions without repeatedly calling on human attention\056 If human attention is the bottleneck\054 genuine micropayments must be designed in a way that doesn\047t consume that attention over and over\056 For a long time\054 though\054 there was no actor capable of evaluating costs and executing payments at the level of individual web requests\056\n\nThe arrival of AI agents has begun to change that\056 Agents don\047t pay mental transaction costs the way humans do\056 For a person\054 deciding whether to spend one cent is a conscious act \024 it involves shifting attention\054 expending cognitive energy\054 accumulating fatigue\056 For an AI agent\054 the same decision is closer to a processing step\072 governed by pre\055assigned goals\054 budgets\054 and policies\054 payment becomes a state transition in a sequence of operations\056 Agents don\047t experience regret when they make a suboptimal call\054 and they don\047t tire from repeating the same decision thousands of times\056 As River Financial put it\072 \042Micropayment theory anticipated high\055frequency\054 low\055value payments\054 but human mental transaction costs limit this behavior\056 AI agents\054 which don\047t bear mental costs\054 could change this dynamic \024 potentially leading to more frequent and smaller payments going forward\056\04222\056\n\nWhat River Financial\047s observation points to is not simply a drop in the unit price of transactions\056 It\047s the possibility that the minimum viable payment unit \024 the floor below which commerce couldn\047t happen \024 is about to be redrawn\056 Under card rails\054 transactions below a few tens of cents were economically unworkable\054 so any value that could only be exchanged in smaller units got absorbed into bundles\072 a monthly subscription at \0449\05699\054 a per\055episode rental at \0441\05699\054 an API plan selling a block of calls at a fixed rate\056 These bundles are convenient \024 users don\047t have to make constant small decisions\054 and providers reduce billing overhead\056 But they also blunt price signals\056 Individual pieces of content\054 API calls\054 and data requests may each carry different value\054 yet the existing payment system couldn\047t express those differences in price\056\n\nThe agent economy opens the possibility of disaggregating those blunt bundles\056 If the party making payment decisions is software rather than a human\054 services can be priced at far finer granularity\056 Among ten thousand API calls\054 some return critical information and some don\047t\056 Some data is only valuable at a specific moment\056 The same API endpoint might reasonably carry different prices depending on response speed and processing priority\056 Agents can read those price signals and execute payment according to preset policies\056 Pricing becomes more precise\054 bundles begin to break apart\054 and the fine\055grained exchanges of supply and demand that were obscured by the blunt instrument of subscription pricing start to emerge\056 This is precisely where HTTP 402 regains its meaning \024 because the code designed to signal \042payment required\042 is finally meeting an actor capable of reading that signal\054 evaluating the price\054 and completing the transaction\056)
     /K [ 0 ]
     /P 15629 0 R
     /Pg 4096 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

