15662 0 obj
  << /ActualText (That wall doesn\047t arise simply because agents find it hard to click screens or enter authentication codes\056 The more fundamental problem is the gap between capability and legal standing\056 An AI agent can be configured to follow the instructions of a specific human user or corporate account\054 and can be delegated a defined scope of tasks\056 But the agent itself is neither a natural person nor a legal entity\056 It has no nationality\054 no national ID or social security number\054 no date of birth\054 and no country will issue it a passport\056 In the financial system\054 accounts and cards are issued to legal subjects to whom responsibility can be assigned\056 An agent can get as far as finding a service\054 comparing options\054 and forming an execution plan \024 but at the moment of payment\054 it must return to the authorization framework of a human or corporate principal\056 An agent\047s autonomy stops the moment a transaction begins\056\n\nNor can this problem be solved simply by granting agents broader payment autonomy\056 The possibility that an agent might execute a payment contrary to the user\047s intent\054 be manipulated by external interference into triggering a fraudulent transaction\054 or generate unintended cumulative spending through repeated calls \024 all of this shows that agent payments immediately become a problem of control\056 For high\055value or high\055stakes transactions \024 cars\054 flights\054 electronics\054 long\055term subscriptions \024 it remains entirely reasonable for the human user\047s explicit approval to be the final step\056 In these cases\054 human intervention is not a bottleneck but a safeguard\056\n\nThe problem is that not all payments are like that\056 For the kinds of payments agents typically need to make \024 API calls\054 data access\054 model usage fees\054 content access\054 compute costs \024 the amounts are small but the frequency is high\056 If every one of these requires a human to open a payment window\054 click approve\054 and enter an OTP\054 the entire point of automation collapses\056 This is where Szabo\047s mental transaction costs resurface\056 The moment you ask a human to evaluate a few cents \024 or less \024 per request\054 the burden of the decision outweighs the value of the payment\056 If micropayments were exhausting and confusing for human users\054 in the agent economy they become precisely the payment type most in need of automation\056\n\nThe practical starting point for agent payments\054 then\054 is not fully autonomous purchasing of high\055value goods\054 but low\055value\054 high\055frequency payments made within a pre\055defined budget and policy\056 The user sets a daily spending cap\054 a maximum per\055call amount\054 a list of permitted services\054 and a list of prohibited transaction types\056 The agent executes the necessary payments automatically within those boundaries\056 Large transactions still require human approval\073 repetitive small ones are handled by the agent\056 This is how human oversight is preserved while meeting the speed and frequency demands of the machine economy\056\n\nCurrent platforms are addressing this within existing payment frameworks\056 Amazon\047s Alexa for Shopping supports product search\054 comparison\054 personalized recommendations\054 price tracking\054 and cart management\05624\056 Its Buy for Me feature pushes agents further into the execution stage \024 handling the purchase process on behalf of the user\054 using their default address and credit card for eligible items\056 But this doesn\047t mean the agent has become an independent payment actor\056 Payment authority remains tied to the human user\047s account\054 address\054 and card\073 the agent is simply delegated to act within a defined scope\056 This is not an overcoming of identity\055based payment \024 it is        \176)
     /K [ 2 ]
     /P 15663 0 R
     /Pg 4610 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

