15675 0 obj
  << /ActualText (The design of that new infrastructure begins with a single shift in perspective\072 redefining the right to execute a payment not as the product of identity verification\054 but as the product of a key\047s ability to sign\056 Blockchain wallets already embody this redefinition\056 A wallet is not created from a national ID\054 a passport\054 or a credit score\056 It operates on the basis of a pair of keys derived from asymmetric cryptography \024 a public key and a private key\056 The public key\054 or an address derived from it\054 serves as an identifier within the network\073 the private key carries the signing authority to transfer assets connected to that address\056 Any entity \024 human\054 machine\054 or organization \024 that can generate a valid key pair and sign with it can initiate transactions on a blockchain network\056 All that is required is a valid signature\056\n\nWhat makes this structure interesting is that it was never designed with AI agents in mind\056 When Satoshi Nakamoto wrote the Bitcoin whitepaper in 2008\054 the problem he was solving was not non\055human payment \024 it was verifying the ownership and transfer of electronic cash without a central authority\056 But the fundamental design he chose \024 a system based on keys rather than identity\054 on protocol rather than permission \024 created a payment environment that was\054 without intending to be\054 open to future actors as well\056 When an AI agent connects to a blockchain network in 2026\054 the network does not ask whether it is human or machine\056 It asks only whether the agent can present a valid signature\056\n\nThis unintended openness of key\055based systems redraws the boundary between the human economy and the machine economy at the level of payment infrastructure\056 The core of the shift is not the arrival of an entirely new currency \024 it is that the prerequisite for payment moves from identity to key\056 Identity\055based payment was the axiom of the human economy\056 Key\055based payment becomes the axiom of the machine economy\056 Just as credit cards and mobile payment services pushed the convenience of identity\055based infrastructure to its limits\054 key\055based protocols like x402 and L402 are what make the machine economy possible in the first place\056\n\nThe two are not in competition where one displaces the other\056 Humans still set the terms of payment responsibility\054 define the boundaries of transactions requiring approval\054 and assign the budgets and policies that agents operate       \176)
     /K [ 4 ]
     /P 15676 0 R
     /Pg 4874 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

