15722 0 obj
  << /ActualText (What makes this design possible is that stablecoins have transformed dollar\055denominated value into a digital token that programs can directly handle\056 A dollar balance in a bank account exists as an entry in a financial institution\047s internal ledger\073 a stablecoin exists as a digital asset that can be transferred between addresses on a blockchain network\056 A program can therefore read a payment request\054 calculate the required amount\054 and execute payment through a wallet signature when the specified conditions are met\056 At the same time\054 because dollar stablecoins are designed to maintain a 1\0721 peg to the dollar\054 the amounts a program handles are interpreted in relatively stable dollar terms \024 a crucial condition for agents to evaluate per\055call costs\054 allocate budgets\054 and automatically judge payment conditions\056\n\nThat said\054 using stablecoins doesn\047t automatically make every payment economically viable at extreme micro\055scales\056 The practical minimum billing unit depends on the chain being used\054 network fees\054 the facilitator\047s cost structure\054 and whether payments are settled per request or batched\056 Even so\054 stablecoin\055based payment opens up design possibilities that card systems cannot\056 Where card payment\047s combination of percentage and flat fees creates a hard floor below which transactions can\047t work\054 stablecoin\055based payment on a low\055fee chain \024 combined with automated verification and usage\055based billing \024 can be designed to work at far smaller units\056\n\nx402\047s basic payment schemes are divided into \042exact\042 and \042upto\056\04226\056 The exact scheme processes a fixed amount as specified \024 suited to APIs or content where the price is set in advance\056 The upto scheme lets the client authorize a maximum amount\054 with the server settling within that cap based on  \176 )
     /K [ 5 ]
     /P 15723 0 R
