15769 0 obj
  << /ActualText (x402 and L402 represent two competing designs for the machine economy\047s payment execution layer\056 But the real ecosystem is not a closed competition between just these two\056 Several recently proposed agent payment protocols are addressing the same problem from different angles \024 focusing on proving authorization and intent\054 and on coordinating payment methods\054 rather than on execution itself\056\n\nWhere x402 and L402 focus on how to execute per\055request payments\054 Google\047s AP2 focuses on how to verify that an agent is acting within the user\047s intent and authorized scope\056 AP2 was put forward as an open protocol for safely initiating and processing agent\055driven payments\054 designed to work alongside A2A \050Agent2Agent\051 for agent interoperability and MCP \050Model Context Protocol\051\055based tool environments\05634\056 It is also designed not to be tied to any specific payment method\054 aiming instead to serve as a trust framework within which users\054 merchants\054 and payment providers can transact reliably across a variety of payment rails\056\n\nStripe and Tempo\047s MPP operates at yet another level\056 Presented as an internet\055native payment standard enabling agents and services to coordinate payments programmatically\054 MPP aims to make per\055request payment possible for resource usage including API calls\054 tool invocations\054 and content access\056       \176)
     /K [ 6 ]
     /P 15770 0 R
     /Pg 6642 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
