15823 0 obj
  << /ActualText (A concept that illuminates stablecoins\047 structural limitations from another angle is counterparty risk\05612\051 Consider a scenario where the agent of Company A and the agent of Company B are transacting\056 If the payment method is a stablecoin chosen by Company A\054 Company B must accept a payment asset tied to Company A\047s preferred infrastructure\056 If that stablecoin is subject to a specific country\047s regulations and depends on a specific issuer\047s reserve health\054 Company B\047s exposure to that risk grows with the size of the transaction\056 When one party\047s chosen asset is bound to a specific issuer and jurisdiction\054 the other party enters the shadow of that control\056\n\nThis structure echoes a familiar scene from the history of international trade\056 Since the mid\055twentieth century\054 a significant portion of international transactions has been settled in dollars\054 giving the United States what has been called the \042exorbitant privilege\056\04213\051 As long as payments are made in dollars\054 final settlement in many cases runs through the US financial system and dollar clearing networks\056 The United States has been able to project the sanctioning power of its currency globally on the basis of this position\056 The financial sanctions imposed on Russia following the annexation of Crimea in 2014 and the invasion of Ukraine in 2022 are among the most prominent examples of this mechanism in action\056 The payment route itself becomes an instrument of politics\056\n\nIn the machine economy\054 the same structure could repeat at far greater speed and far finer granularity\056 In an economy where millions of agents transact across dozens of different companies and jurisdictions\054 a payment system dependent on a single issuer or a single country\047s regulations becomes a single point of failure\056 As that economy grows large enough\054 demand will naturally emerge for assets that belong to no particular party \024 just as it did in the human economy\056 The machine economy\054 like the human economy before it\054 will need something that functions as a reserve currency\056 So what will that asset be\077)
     /K [ 6 ]
     /P 15824 0 R
     /Pg 7696 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
