15840 0 obj
  << /ActualText (slow and expensive\054 but it belongs to no one and is no one\047s liability\056 The dollar and the euro\054 by contrast\054 are fast and convenient but tied to the institutional credit of states and central banks\056 Gold and fiat don\047t replace each other \024 they serve different functions\056\n\nThe same division of labor is likely to take hold in the machine economy\056 Stablecoins will handle the bulk of payment transactions \024 the frequent\054 small exchanges involved in service calls\054 API billing\054 content access\054 and similar activity will largely be processed on dollar\055pegged stablecoins like USDC\056 But for stablecoin reserves\054 corporate collateral settlement\054 and the final settlement layer of cross\055border agent transactions\054 a different kind of asset may be needed\072 one not controlled by any single company or subject to any single government\047s regulatory reach \024 an asset whose neutrality is built into the protocol itself\056\n\nAs of 2026\054 the asset that comes closest to meeting those conditions is Bitcoin\056 The Bitcoin base layer is slow\054 its price is volatile\054 and it has real limitations as a medium of everyday payment\056 But Bitcoin is not the liability of any company and not the asset of any nation\056 Its maximum supply is capped at 21 million\054 and changing that rule would require broad consensus among network participants\056 This neutrality is not a side feature \024 it is a design principle\056 In 2014\054 venture capitalist Marc Andreessen placed Bitcoin alongside the personal computer of 1975 and the internet of 1993 as an example of technological potential that the mainstream had not yet grasped\05639\056\n\nThe prospect of Bitcoin emerging as the machine economy\047s reserve asset is already being discussed seriously in parts of the industry\056 BlackRock CEO Larry Fink\054 in his 2025 annual letter to shareholders\054 warned that if the United States failed to control its debt and fiscal deficits continued to expand\054 the dollar\047s reserve currency status could be threatened by digital assets like Bitcoin\05640\056 Tether CEO Paolo Ardoino\054 in a June 2025 interview\054 argued that JP Morgan would not be opening bank accounts for AI agents\054 and that agents would end up transacting with stablecoins and Bitcoin \024 projecting a future within fifteen years in which one trillion agents settle transactions using blockchain\055based assets\05641\056 One argument starts from the macro variable of national debt\073 the other from the micro infrastructure of machine payments \024 but both point in a similar direction\056 Stablecoins solve the speed and unit\055of\055account problem\054 but as long as they depend on an issuer\047s reserves and redemption promise\054 they cannot fully escape the issuer\047s creditworthiness and regulatory environment\056 As the machine economy grows\054 demand for a neutral settlement asset not tied to any issuing party is likely to grow with it\056 Fink and Ardoino\047s comments reflect less a specific protocol endorsement than a growing awareness within the industry that as the machine economy expands\054 separating the payment layer from the reserve layer becomes increasingly important\056\n\nThe separation of payment layer and reserve layer is likely to become the central design principle of the machine economy\047s long\055term settlement architecture\056 At the execution level\054 protocols like x402 and L402 handle everyday transactions using stablecoins and Lightning payments\056 At the reserve and final settlement level\054 Bitcoin is called upon\056 This is not a race in which stablecoins and Bitcoin compete for a single winner\056 It is closer to the formation of an architecture in which the two assets handle different tiers\056 How that division of labor settles into specific standards and institutional                     \176)
     /K [ 2 ]
     /P 15841 0 R
     /Pg 7886 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
