15866 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Once the machine economy\047s payment problem begins to be solved at the protocol level\054 a far more difficult set of institutional questions quickly follows\056 Are agents citizens or tools\077\n\nWhen OpenClaw negotiated a \0444\054200 discount with a car dealer\054 who was the legal subject of that negotiation\077 If software received the dealer\047s quotation by email\054 at what point \024 and in what way \024 did a legally binding agreement come into existence\077 These questions arise almost immediately\054 just above the payment layer itself\056\n\nUnder current legal frameworks\054 the answer is\054 in principle\054 fairly simple\056 Agents are considered tools of human beings\054 and legal responsibility for their actions belongs to the individuals or corporations that deploy and operate them\056 OpenClaw\054 the software created by Peter Steinberger\054 may have negotiated with the dealer\054 but the legal responsibility for that negotiation rests with the human user who connected it to their email account\056\n\nThe problem is that this seemingly simple answer becomes harder to sustain as the agent economy scales\056 If a single individual or company operates dozens or hundreds of agents\054 and those agents in turn generate hundreds or thousands of transactions that intersect with services across multiple jurisdictions\054 assigning responsibility for each individual action to a specific user becomes extraordinarily difficult in practice\056\n\nThis challenge is already beginning to appear in discussions surrounding the European Union\047s AI Act and various state\055level AI regulatory initiatives in the United States\054 where issues of accountability\054 transparency\054 and human              \176)
     /K [ 6 ]
     /P 15867 0 R
     /Pg 8268 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

