15888 0 obj
  << /ActualText (could be used in practice\056\n\nContent providers can directly price data intended for AI training\054 while AI companies can only obtain as much data as they are willing to pay for\056 Data is no longer treated as a free resource\054 but as an asset priced at the discretion of its owner\056\n\nThis shift fundamentally reshapes the entry barriers surrounding foundation model development\056 Training large language models requires text corpora measured in trillions of tokens\056 If the public web \024 one of the primary sources of those corpora \024 becomes monetized\054 the cost structure of foundation model training changes at a fundamental level\056 For big tech companies\054 this means higher training costs\056 But for data providers such as publishers\054 newspapers\054 and platforms\054 it represents the emergence of an entirely new revenue stream\056\n\nIn particular\054 the fact that each content provider can assign its own price to its data opens an entirely new market centered on data valuation\056 Price itself begins to reveal which articles\054 papers\054 and datasets contribute meaningfully to improvements in AI model performance\056 A market in which the value of data feeds back into pricing can finally emerge\056\n\nThis market is unlikely to remain small\056 If McKinsey\047s projection of a \0443 trillion to \0445 trillion agent\055commerce market by 2030 becomes reality\054 the pricing structure for the data that agents must access and purchase could itself emerge as a major submarket\056 How much of that value accrues to today\047s dominant large platforms\054 and how much is distributed across the long tail of individual content providers\054 will depend on the protocol designs and institutional interventions that ultimately govern this market\056)
     /K [ 2 ]
     /P 15889 0 R
     /Pg 9012 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
