15916 0 obj
  << /ActualText (We began with OpenClaw\047s car negotiation case and Moltbook\047s AI agent experiments\054 examining why HTTP 402 \024 a code dormant for thirty years \024 is now resurfacing\056 As we explored the limitations on micropayments imposed by card payment networks\054 what Nick Szabo described as mental transaction costs\054 the shift in payment authorization from identity\055based systems to key\055based systems\054 and the payment layer of the machine economy emerging through x402 and L402\054 one conclusion became increasingly clear\072 payment is no longer an activity reserved solely for humans\056\n\nIf payment becomes native to the web\054 the logic of the internet economy itself begins to change\056 Until now\054 the web has primarily functioned as infrastructure for the exchange of information through requests and responses\056 The next generation of the web may embed transfers of value directly into those requests and responses\056 Website resources\054 API calls\054 data access\054 and intellectual property usage can each carry a price\054 while agents can interpret those prices and execute payments within predefined budgets and policy constraints\056 In this environment\054 micropayments are no longer viewed as an old failed experiment\054 but as a foundational mechanism of the machine economy\056\n\nBut this future will not arrive automatically\056 If the payment layer of the machine economy becomes excessively concentrated around a single company or a particular stablecoin\054 we may simply repeat an old problem in a new form \024 one in which centralized services can unilaterally change the rules while users remain dependent on them\056 The dangers of centralization that Vitalik Buterin once saw in the skill nerfs of World of Warcraft could reappear within the payment layer of the machine economy as well\056\n\nAvoiding this outcome will require a multilayered architecture in which dependence on any single asset or payment rail cannot destabilize the system as a whole\056 If the payment layer and the reserve layer are separated\054 if the convenience of stablecoins and the neutrality of Bitcoin can coexist at different layers\054 and if the identity\055based accountability systems of the human economy operate in parallel with the key\055based execution systems of the machine economy\054 then the resulting infrastructure may prove more stable\056\n\nJust as HTTP became the language of information that built the web\054 it remains uncertain whether x402 can become the language of value for the next generation of the internet\056\n\nThirty years from now\054 how will x402 be remembered\077 Will it be remembered merely as the name of a payment service tied to a particular era\054 like PayPal\077 Or will it become a foundational component of internet infrastructure\054 like TCP\057IP\077 The answer to that question will not be determined by technical superiority alone\056 The governance of the foundation\054 the ability of participating companies to reach consensus\054 the attitudes of regulators around the world\054 and above all the choices made by both human and machine users will determine its direction\056)
     /K [ 1 ]
     /P 15917 0 R
     /Pg 9324 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

